; Hints.inix -- what each control in a DbDo dialog does, in one sentence, spoken by Shift+F1.
; A section is a dialog's title as its window shows it; a key is a control's name, without its
; underline letter or trailing colon; add the kind in parentheses, as in "Search (Button)", only when
; two controls of one dialog share a name. [All dialogs] holds names that mean the same everywhere.
; A control with no hint here says the first sentence of its own description instead.
; Your own copy, in %LOCALAPPDATA%\DbDo\configs, wins over this one, so you can change a hint.

[All dialogs]
OK = Saves what you have chosen and closes the dialog; Control+Enter does this from any control.
Cancel = Closes the dialog and changes nothing; Escape does this from any control.
Help = Opens this dialog's help, which F1 also does from any control.
Close = Closes this window.
Any field = Finds records holding some text in any field.
Field boxes = Gives a box for each field, where a value and an operator such as >= or a range such as 2025..2026 can be typed.
Marked records = Keeps the records you have marked.
Unmarked records = Keeps the records you have not marked.
Records = The table's records, one to a row; arrows move by record and by column, and Enter opens the record.

[Select Columns]
Look columns = Shows look's columns in look's order, the default the table opens with; with no look, every main column.
Prime columns = Shows prime's columns in prime's order: the default sort order, and together the values unique to each record.
Main columns = Shows only the columns about the table's subject, leaving out url, tags, notes and the other standard columns.
Standard columns = Shows only the standard columns: url, tags, notes, marked, look, prime, added, edited and the id.
All columns = Shows every column of the table.
Choose columns = Lets you pick the columns one by one, in the order they should appear.
Type columns = Lets you type the column names, separated by commas, in the order they should appear.
Columns available = The columns not yet shown, in DbDo's natural order; Enter adds the one you are on to the end of Columns shown.
Add column = Adds the column selected in Columns available to the end of Columns shown.
Columns shown = The grid's columns in the order they will appear; Enter or Delete puts one back.
Remove column = Takes the column selected in Columns shown out of it and back into Columns available.
Empty list = Clears the list at once, so OK returns to the default.

[Order Records]
Fields available = The fields not yet in the sort, in DbDo's natural order; Enter adds the one you are on, ascending.
Add ascending = Adds the selected field to the sort, smallest or earliest first.
Add descending = Adds the selected field to the sort, largest or latest first.
Order of fields = The sort, first field first; each reads like city asc, and Enter or Delete takes one out.
Prime order = Sorts by prime's fields in prime's order, ascending: the table's default order, which also gives each record a place of its own.
Look order = Sorts by look's fields in look's order, ascending: the way records are said.
Choose order = Lets you pick the fields and the direction of each, one by one.
Type order = Lets you type the fields separated by commas, with desc after a field to sort it largest or latest first.
Reverse order = Turns each field of the order in force the other way: ascending becomes descending.

[Type Order]
Fields in order = The fields to sort by, first field first, separated by commas; desc after a field sorts it largest or latest first.
Remove field = Takes the field selected in Order of fields out of the sort.
Empty list = Clears the sort at once, so OK returns to the default order.

[Filter Records]
Start over = Begins a new filter, replacing the one in force.
Clear filter = Removes the filter, so every record shows again.
Narrow filter = Keeps only the records that also meet a new condition.
Expand filter = Also takes in the records that meet a new condition.
Invert filter = Shows the records this filter leaves out, and hides the ones it keeps.
Edit filter = Opens the filter in force so its values can be changed.
Any field = Finds records holding some text in any field.
Marked records = Keeps the records you have marked.
Unmarked records = Keeps the records you have not marked.
Field boxes = Gives a box for each field, where a value and an operator such as >= or a range such as 2025..2026 can be typed.

[Filter Fields]
Main fields = Gives a box for each field about the table's subject.
Standard fields = Gives a box for url, tags, notes, marked, look, prime, added, edited and the id.
All fields = Gives a box for every field of the table.

[Add Table]
Define table = Starts a new table from blank, naming its fields one by one.
Clone fields = Starts a new table with the same fields as the table now open, and none of its records.

[Update from Template]
Merge template = Adds what the template has and your copy lacks, keeping everything of yours.
Replace copy = Makes your copy the template again, keeping your old copy beside it as a dated backup.

[Merge Database]
Add new only = Adds records this database lacks, and leaves every record already here as it is.
Update records = Adds new records, and replaces each matching record here with the other database's.
Fill blanks = Adds new records, and fills only the empty fields of matching records here.
Newer wins = Adds new records, and for a matching record keeps whichever was edited last.

[List Bookmarks]
Bookmarks saved = Your bookmarks, by table and record; Enter goes to the one you are on.

[Clear Bookmark]
Bookmarks saved = Your bookmarks, by table and record; pick one to clear, or clear them all.

[Edit Cell]
Field value = The value of this field in this record; Control+Enter saves it.

[Database Summary]
Database summary = The tables of this database, their fields and record counts; read it by line, or copy it.

[Graphics Grid]
Chart column = The column to chart; the chart shows how often each value appears in the records shown.

[Invoke Script]
Script name = The scripts you can run; Enter runs the one you are on.

[Edit Script]
Script name = The scripts you can open for editing; Enter opens the one you are on.
